Moving from a Monorepo to a Polyrepo (Multiple Repositories) for your GMS (Game Management System) changes the architecture from one "big box" to a "modular kit."In a Polyrepo setup, you would split your project into independent repositories. Here is how that looks for your GMS:1. The Repository SplitRepo 1: GMS-Dashboard (The web/desktop UI for parameters).Repo 2: GMS-Unity-Core (The C# scripts and logic that live inside Unity).Repo 3: GMS-Schemas (The shared JSON definitions for your 3D Scene payloads).Repo 4: The-Game (The actual game project that imports the GMS).2. Benefits for your "Vibe Coding" & Battery IssuesClean Context: An AI agent (or Battery Management system) looking at the "The-Game" repo will never see the dashboard code. The "vibe" is isolated to the specific task of that repo.Independent Scaling: You can update the Dashboard UI (Repo 1) without triggering a re-compile or a "build" of the actual Game (Repo 4).Strict Contracts: Since they are in different repos, you are forced to define exactly how the 3D Scene Payload looks. This prevents "spaghetti code" between the dashboard and the engine.3. How to connect them (The Unity Way)In a Polyrepo, you don't copy-paste code. You use the Unity Package Manager (UPM).You turn your GMS-Unity-Core into a package and add it to your game’s manifest.json via Git URL:json"dependencies": {
  "com.yourcompany.gms": "https://github.com"
}
